From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
Date: Thu, 21 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: recore: keep U-Boot's routine output and logo off the panel

The running commentary U-Boot prints does not belong on a printer's front
panel. Until now the default environment put the video console in stdout, so
from console_init_r onwards every line was drawn on the display: the
In:/Out:/Err: banner, the network probe, USB enumeration, the autoboot
countdown and the mmc scan - roughly twenty lines over about five seconds - and
only then did the boot script draw boot.bmp on top of it.

That is what #84 is about. boot.bmp is 480x480 and centred, U-Boot has no
scaler, and the panel is much larger, so the splash never covered the text
around it. The boot script now clears the screen before drawing, which fixes
the overlap, but the text is still there to be seen for the seconds before it.
Not putting it on the panel in the first place is the actual fix; the clear
stays as a safety net for a board whose eMMC still holds an older U-Boot.

The video device itself is unaffected. stdio_add_devices() probes UCLASS_VIDEO
unconditionally, independently of what stdout names, and video_post_probe()
clears the framebuffer - so the panel comes up black (CONFIG_SYS_WHITE_ON_BLACK
is set), the framebuffer handover to the kernel's simpledrm is unchanged, and
`bmp display` still works because it talks to the video uclass rather than to
the console. The boot script re-binds vidconsole for the single `cls` call it
needs, because with CONFIG_VIDEO_ANSI=y cls clears by printing an escape
sequence rather than by touching the device.

Dropping CONFIG_VIDEO_LOGO removes the other thing on the panel, which is not
the console and so survives the change above. Armbian's boot-splash patch puts
"splashimage=66000000" and "splashpos=m,m" in the default environment, and
stdio_add_devices() calls splash_display() as soon as the video device is
probed - long before the boot script runs. With no CONFIG_SPLASH_SOURCE the
weak splash_screen_prepare() falls through to splash_video_logo_load(), which
copies U-Boot's built-in 80x80 logo to that address, and bmp_display() draws it
centred on the panel. Ours covers it five seconds later, at the same address
and also centred, so it has only ever been a brief flash of someone else's
branding. Without VIDEO_LOGO, splash_video_logo_load() returns -ENOSYS,
splash_display() gives up, and the panel stays as video_post_probe() left it.
CONFIG_VIDEO_BMP_RLE8 is select'ed by VIDEO_LOGO, but recore_defconfig also
sets it explicitly, so nothing is lost.

Silent while it is going well, loud when it is not. That needs three things,
because a boot can fail in three different places, and only one of them is
somewhere the boot script can reach:

1. stderr deliberately keeps vidconsole. A panel that goes black and stays
   black tells a user nothing, so the few things U-Boot does route to stderr
   should still reach it - on this board that is sunxi_de2.c's three "Cannot
   setup simplefb" failures, which are worth seeing precisely because they mean
   Linux is about to come up with no display. It is a thin channel: everything
   else, including "Unknown command", failed loads and hush syntax errors, goes
   through printf() to stdout.

2. CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND wraps distro_bootcmd. distro_bootcmd only returns when it
   has run out of boot targets, so reaching the end of it means nothing on this
   board was bootable - which is the one failure that happens before any boot
   script exists to report it. Put the video console back and say so.

   This is not hypothetical. On a cold boot on 2026-08-21 the eMMC answered
   `Card did not respond to voltage select! : -110` from mmc_start_init(), and
   with no SD slot and no USB stick still enumerating, U-Boot fell through to
   PXE. Without this, that board is a black panel.

3. The boot script does the rest, once it is running: it puts the video console
   back and names the file on any load it sees fail.

stdin is left as serial,usbkbd. A USB keyboard is little use with no visible
console, but leaving the line alone keeps this diff to one line.

Signed-off-by: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
---
 configs/recore_defconfig       | 3 ++-
 include/configs/sunxi-common.h | 2 +-
 2 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/configs/recore_defconfig b/configs/recore_defconfig
--- a/configs/recore_defconfig
+++ b/configs/recore_defconfig
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
 CONFIG_LED=y
 CONFIG_LED_GPIO=y
 CONFIG_SPLASH_SCREEN_ALIGN=y
-CONFIG_VIDEO_LOGO=y
+# CONFIG_VIDEO_LOGO is not set
 CONFIG_SPLASH_SCREEN=y
 CONFIG_BMP_16BPP=y
 CONFIG_BMP_24BPP=y
@@ -33,3 +33,4 @@
 CONFIG_USE_PREBOOT=y
 CONFIG_PREBOOT="mw.l 0x01f01400 0x00020102; usb reset; run bootcmd_usb0"
 CONFIG_CMD_BMP=y
+CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND="run distro_bootcmd; setenv stdout serial,vidconsole; echo; echo 'BOOT FAILED: nothing to boot from'; echo 'The eMMC did not answer. Connect the serial header for detail.'"
diff --git a/include/configs/sunxi-common.h b/include/configs/sunxi-common.h
--- a/include/configs/sunxi-common.h
+++ b/include/configs/sunxi-common.h
@@ -315,8 +315,8 @@

 #ifdef CONFIG_VIDEO
 #define CONSOLE_STDOUT_SETTINGS \
-	"stdout=serial,vidconsole\0" \
+	"stdout=serial\0" \
 	"stderr=serial,vidconsole\0"
 #else
 #define CONSOLE_STDOUT_SETTINGS \
 	"stdout=serial\0" \
